{
  "name": "How Many Coats of Mud Do I Really Need for Drywall?",
  "step": [
    {
      "name": "Step 1",
      "text": "When you're finishing drywall, the question of 'how many coats' often comes up, and it's a super important one for getting a flawless look. Generally, for taped seams and screw dimples, you're looking at a minimum of three coats of joint compound, often called 'mud.' Here's a breakdown of why:",
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "position": 1
    },
    {
      "name": "Step 2",
      "text": "**Coat 1: The Embed Coat**",
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "position": 2
    },
    {
      "name": "Step 3",
      "text": "This is the initial layer that covers your joint tape or fills those screw dimples. Its main job is to embed the tape securely and fill the deepest parts of the dimples. You'll want it to be firm but not too thick.",
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "position": 3
    },
    {
      "name": "Step 4",
      "text": "**Coat 2: The Fill Coat**",
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "position": 4
    },
    {
      "name": "Step 5",
      "text": "After the first coat is completely dry (give it about 24 hours!), you'll apply a wider, slightly thinner second coat. This coat starts to feather out the edges, building up the surface to be flush with the surrounding drywall. It helps to smooth out any imperfections from the first coat.",
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "position": 5
    },
    {
      "name": "Step 6",
      "text": "**Coat 3: The Finish Coat**",
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "position": 6
    },
    {
      "name": "Step 7",
      "text": "Once your second coat is dry and sanded, the third coat is all about achieving that perfect, seamless finish. This is the widest coat, feathered out even further, to blend everything perfectly with the wall. It conceals any remaining tape lines or slight depressions. For a truly professional look, sometimes a fourth, very light skim coat is used, especially if you're going for a Level 5 finish. Skipping any of these steps often leads to visible seams and fasteners, or areas that 'flash' when painted. If you want a really smooth finish that stands up to scrutiny, HH Remodeling LLC can deliver that expert drywall finishing.",
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "position": 7
    }
  ],
  "@type": "HowTo",
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "speakable": {
    "@type": "SpeakableSpecification",
    "cssSelector": [
      "h1",
      ".howto-step",
      "[itemprop=\"step\"]"
    ]
  },
